e out and  got us now we've popped out the other  side and we're back in the forecast wind  so uh looking good heading east at pace  it's probably closer to a dive  expedition we we spend a lot of time  underwater all of our gear is our only  line of defense from  becoming totally saturated turning into  a giant raisin you've got 18 guys on  board and it's for five and a half days  so it's pretty tight quarters even  though it is a hundred foot boat you're  sharing bunks sharing bowls you're  sharing water bottles for drinking and  everything for the two groups of eight  we do a four hour on four hour system  this race we just did a simple four on  four off you know traditional watch  system  that's a challenge in itself you know  waking up every four hours to come on  deck and do your watch  and then off you go again you might  sleep for three hours out of that when  you're down when you've eaten  if you're lucky you get your four hours  off watch but you never miss your four  hours on deck and when you're off watch  you got to do everything else it's when  you've got to do the cooking do the  tidying up do the eating  and try to get some sleep the four hours  goes by pretty darn quick  so you have your helmsman who are  they'll drive the boat for two hours  you've got your trimmers of the sails  who are basically the engine making the  boat go fast so i'm one of the uh the  bright individuals that is on the front  of the boat that's basically taking on a  lot of the dangerous situations up front  we deal with all the sails going up and  down and then you've got a lot of the  guys who are required for the mussels  and everything to pull those sails in  the more prestigious of the records are  set by boats where all of the sail  handling  is done with human power we have six  brining pedestals so we have to pull any  sails up or trim them in we have 12 guys  just going at it until you know the trim  is happy or you know the sales up  you're working the sales you're 